WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.321
(bright music)

00:00:01.321 --> 00:00:02.560
(house cracks)
- Casita?

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.140
(house rumbles)

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.824
What's going on?

00:00:05.824 --> 00:00:06.850
(bird screeches)

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:08.606
- The magic is in danger.

00:00:08.606 --> 00:00:10.468
We gotta get out of here!

00:00:10.468 --> 00:00:12.180
- We must protect our home.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.296
We must protect our family.

00:00:14.296 --> 00:00:15.150
(bright music)

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.940
- [Jared] So, "Encanto"
is an animated musical

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.270
set in Colombia

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.490
and it's about this amazing family.

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.450
All of them have these
wonderful, magical gifts,

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.317
with the exception of the
youngest granddaughter, Mirabel,

00:00:28.317 --> 00:00:31.590
and the story is about her
learning to see herself

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:32.880
and her family different.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.140
- Hello everybody, I'm Byron Howard,

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.090
one of the directors of "Encanto"

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:37.420
which we'll be talking about today.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:38.910
- And I'm Jared Bush,

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.660
another director of
"Encanto" and co-writer.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.570
And today we're gonna be looking at a clip

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.220
towards the end of the film,

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.330
which is Mirabel at her lowest moment

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:49.070
where for the first time

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:52.300
she starts to understand her
grandmother, Abuela Alma,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:53.650
a little differently

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:56.170
and learns to understand the
choices that she has made

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:57.643
over the course of her life.

00:00:58.746 --> 00:01:01.496
(Mirabel crying)

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.530
All right, so here we're with Mirabel

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.830
and what's pretty much her lowest moment.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.470
The house has just fallen.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.580
It was really important to us
that Mirabel blamed herself

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.260
for everything that's just happened.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.810
To have Mirabel take it so hard

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.200
to really see that as
her singular failure,

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.470
we don't know how we're going
to ever come back from it.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.760
So when Abuela Alma steps in here

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.370
she immediately assumes
that Abuela Alma is there

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.427
to reprimand or to scold her.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:34.290
- I'm sorry.

00:01:37.213 --> 00:01:39.130
I didn't wanna hurt us.

00:01:40.613 --> 00:01:43.331
I just wanted to...

00:01:43.331 --> 00:01:45.930
To be something I'm not.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.450
- This sequence was a combination

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.630
of many different people, myself included,

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.650
drawing many, many different
versions of this moment,

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.850
and I think we all
really in the story rooms

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:57.520
we all get in the same page emotionally,

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.247
like we all kind of
know what the target is.

00:01:59.247 --> 00:02:02.140
And we had talked about
this scene for years

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.420
during the development of "Encanto"

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.720
because it was really
the most critical moment

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.343
between these two women.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:15.933
- I've never been able to come back here.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:19.493
This river

00:02:21.591 --> 00:02:24.480
is where we were given our miracle.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.810
- Knowing that it's a musical

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:28.120
we spent a lot of time in this moment

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:30.910
with what should it sound like.

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:33.080
We dropped out the music completely,

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.400
even the environment sounds are low,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.120
muted colors, we looked at even
like the speed of the river.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.920
We really wanted things to feel very still

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.250
and honestly for there to be
a little bit of discomfort

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.635
in how bald and how naked this moment is.

00:02:47.635 --> 00:02:49.552
- Where Abuelo Pedro...

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.603
- I thought we would
have a different life.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:01.373
I thought I would be a different woman.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.840
- We rarely ask voice actors
to put on a funny voice,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.540
we really want people
to be those characters

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.110
and to really relate to those characters,

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.070
and for Maria Cecilia
Botero she's Colombian

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.850
so she knows what this moment is saying.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:19.700
She knows about the resilience
and the pain and the strength

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.620
that this moment requires

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.041
and what her own grandmother
and grandfather went through.

00:03:25.041 --> 00:03:27.659
♪ Dos oruguitas ♪

00:03:27.659 --> 00:03:28.600
♪ Enamoradas ♪

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:30.760
- As the non-songwriters you go like,

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.760
this feels like it could be
a musical moment, you know,

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.740
something we always talk about is,

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.530
why does there have to be a song there?

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:37.430
Usually it's because

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:39.670
there's something very
emotional that's happening,

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.730
that a character can't
just say those things,

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.270
they must be compelled
to sing those things.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:45.660
Once we had the sense of like

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.220
maybe this is a song that
a character is not singing,

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:49.210
we're hearing it,

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:50.340
it is that sort of,

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.700
it sounds like an old folk song

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.490
or it might sound like this is a song

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.760
that the spirit of Abuelo Pedro

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.550
is somehow singing a little bit

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:59.915
started to make a lot of sense to us.

00:03:59.915 --> 00:04:01.698
♪ Siguen andando ♪

00:04:01.698 --> 00:04:04.017
♪ Y navegando un mundo ♪

00:04:04.017 --> 00:04:04.927
♪ Que cambia y sigue ♪

00:04:04.927 --> 00:04:07.520
- [Byron] The wonderful thing
that happens during this song

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:08.500
is that it's all visual,

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:11.480
it has a beautiful love
story of this young couple

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.000
who had all the hope in the world

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.820
and things went very, very differently

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.090
than they had expected.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:18.980
The research that our
teams did on materials,

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:20.820
like every loop of that embroidery

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.710
and the fabric on the bed is
so beautifully researched,

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:26.320
every tile is painted some of it by hand.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.499
So it's really these movies are handmade.

00:04:29.499 --> 00:04:32.030
♪ El tiempo sigue cambiando ♪

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:32.940
♪ Inseparables son ♪

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.750
- [Jared] It was really
critical for us to show

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:35.960
the youth of these characters

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:37.640
before what we're seeing now

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.280
which is the tragedy of their history

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.830
starting to come into play,

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:44.610
bringing in some of
the history of violence

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.500
that many people in Colombia had faced,

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.130
and that was very critical to them

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.080
to make sure that that
was included in the story.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.350
There's an amazing amount of dissonance.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:56.260
The song is telling you

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:57.820
that it's not going in a great direction

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:59.090
but you're seeing something wonderful

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:00.180
and that tells you like, uh-oh,

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:01.210
I'm starting to not feel good.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.750
And then you're seeing
things get worse and worse

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.870
and then extremely tragic

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:07.440
as you're still hearing a love song.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.970
That combination was something

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.110
that I think really was the efforts

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:14.860
of every department
firing on all cylinders,

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.370
at their very best to deliver something

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:20.047
that is greater than the sum of its parts.

00:05:20.047 --> 00:05:21.419
♪ Aparte y volver ♪

00:05:21.419 --> 00:05:23.662
♪ Hacia adelante seguiras ♪

00:05:23.662 --> 00:05:25.943
♪ Vienen milagros ♪

00:05:25.943 --> 00:05:28.591
♪ Vienen crisalidas ♪

00:05:28.591 --> 00:05:33.591
♪ Hay que partir y
construir su propio futuro ♪

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.830
- When the miracle happened,
Byron boarded this,

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:38.460
the candle itself sort of exploded

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:42.060
with a butterfly of flame
came out of the candle

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.860
and it was this enormous
eight-foot-wide butterfly

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:49.940
that used its wings to
blow back the assailants

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:51.010
that were coming on their horses,

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:52.930
and it was this really spectacular thing

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:55.000
that was in our film for quite a long time

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:56.060
'cause it was so beautiful.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:58.600
Ultimately it felt like, okay, well,

00:05:58.600 --> 00:05:59.990
that's a wonderful moment

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:01.290
but we needed to simplify it.

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:02.670
The neat thing was

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.420
we then had this butterfly imagery

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:05.440
that was already in our heads,

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:07.980
so as Lin went to write this song

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.240
in his mind he had the
greatest miracle of nature,

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:13.370
which is that transformation
from caterpillar to butterfly.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:15.380
Sometimes these pieces come in and out

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.730
but they can inform you in a
way that leads you down a path

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:19.810
that you never could have gotten to

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:20.930
had you not done the early work,

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:22.610
even though that early work changes.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:25.370
- [Byron] Alma decides
that she has to leave

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:26.500
this pain behind

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.210
and push it down and become someone else,

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:31.857
and this is I think Mirabel
actually seeing that

00:06:31.857 --> 00:06:34.410
and her grandmother
and seeing her journey.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.650
- [Jared] It's a fantasy
space and we do play a lot

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.450
with staying true to
emotionally what's going on,

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:42.840
but, you know, it is a
bit of a risk, right?

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.110
We're changing the
dimensions of the house,

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:46.395
that's kind of weird,

00:06:46.395 --> 00:06:49.050
the doors are not in the
order that you've seen them in

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.040
throughout the course of the movie,

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.690
but emotionally it felt right.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:52.910
But those are risks you take

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:55.710
hoping that the audience will
go with you on that journey.

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:57.350
And then you're also watching her age

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:59.960
and we didn't want her age to feel extreme

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:03.030
and yet we wanted this all
to feel very lyrical and soft

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:04.250
as you're watching it

00:07:04.250 --> 00:07:06.840
so that you're just really
tracking the emotion

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:07.673
through all this

00:07:07.673 --> 00:07:10.917
leading to this moment where
she gets to young Mirabel

00:07:10.917 --> 00:07:12.970
and the moment where
she didn't get a gift,

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:15.290
and this is the first
time we see this reaction

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.180
from Abuela Alma where she's realizing

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:19.020
how she let Mirabel down,

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:20.480
how she should have been
there to comfort her

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:22.273
and how she really lost her way.

00:07:27.424 --> 00:07:30.757
(wings flapping softly)

00:07:34.039 --> 00:07:35.643
(gentle music)

00:07:35.643 --> 00:07:36.476
- Abuela.

00:07:39.263 --> 00:07:40.363
I can finally see.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:42.960
- [Byron] So coming out of the song

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.067
we talked a lot about what
Mirabel is feeling in that moment

00:07:45.067 --> 00:07:46.610
and what she would say,

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:49.330
and I love that it's sort of wordless,

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:51.800
I don't think she knows what
to say to her grandmother.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.820
- [Jared] And I love this
part so, so much, you know?

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:56.290
I think this is an area where

00:07:56.290 --> 00:08:00.060
we start to lean into Germaine
Franco's beautiful score

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.950
where you're hearing the "Encanto" theme,

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.340
the theme of the miracle,

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:05.730
and it's the miracle
thing that's happening

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:08.450
as Mirabel is reacting with empathy

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:10.880
and she's seeing her
grandmother for the first time.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:13.480
- I love how beautifully
subtle the acting is

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:15.530
and even the tiny little things

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:19.552
like how much the tears are
showing up in their eyes,

00:08:19.552 --> 00:08:20.690
'cause it's easy to go too far.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.550
But I just think when I saw this coming in

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.140
and it was being animated,

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.370
I believe in these people,

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:28.810
I believe that we're watching
real people with real souls

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:32.260
and it's such a magnificent
emotional moment.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:34.640
- I think Andrew Ford
animated this moment,

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:35.820
if I'm not mistaken.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:38.070
He animated both sides of both characters

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:39.840
having this conversation

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:42.780
and didn't worry about
camera placement yet.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:44.660
So often in animation

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.240
you're with the character that's speaking,

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.660
but we wanted to be with the
character that was feeling

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:52.150
and to see that emotion hitting her face,

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:54.650
and so after we actually
had the performance

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:59.650
then we chose who to be on
camera for what part of that.

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:01.850
We knew we were driving to this moment,

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.440
we knew that the sun was gonna come out,

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:05.273
they would be in the middle of this river,

00:09:05.273 --> 00:09:07.850
and that they would be
surrounded by butterflies.

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:10.310
And we spent a ton of time figuring out

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:12.690
how many butterflies did we need,

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:15.100
and we kept on adding
literally hundreds more

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:16.400
and hundreds more,

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:18.630
and what are they doing and
what's the murmuration look like

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:20.810
and does it head back,
does it not head back?

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.420
But I think I remember
every single time we saw it

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:24.150
we were like, "Double the butterflies."

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:24.983
And then we saw it again and we were like,

00:09:24.983 --> 00:09:25.816
"This is way too many,"

00:09:25.816 --> 00:09:26.970
then we're like, "No, double it again."

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:29.720
- It's not just an effect to
put butterflies in the air

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:31.040
but to kind of how do you support that

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:34.450
and make it feel like
this extraordinary moment.

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:36.950
- It's also in the middle
of the Cano Cristales

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:39.970
which is this amazing
real river in Colombia.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:41.670
That is literally what it looks like.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:44.120
You went there, that is exactly right.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.760
And that's something that
I think a lot of people

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:48.587
from Colombia who know it were like,

00:09:48.587 --> 00:09:50.350
"I know exactly what that is."

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:52.570
And hopefully people that watch the movie

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:54.680
want to then go experience
that for themselves,

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:56.900
discovering that this thing
that looks so fantastical

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:58.120
is a real place.

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.210
- I've seen this scene
for more than two years

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:01.520
and I still get emotional watching it.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:04.390
I think that everyone
who touched this scene

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:05.360
did such an amazing job

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:06.620
and I think brought
their own emotion to it,

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:09.780
so it's a huge compliment
to our filmmaking team

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:11.810
and it's just a wonderful song too,

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:13.230
I just absolutely love this song.

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.650
- For me, I have a really
hard time separating

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:17.710
that journey of creating it

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:20.640
and the journey of Mirabel and Abuela Alma

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.920
who emotionally are coming together.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.900
I don't know that I'll
ever be able to separate it

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:29.070
but I do love seeing people
that have watched this

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:31.040
and it's emotional to a lot of people

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:33.564
'cause I think it really
resonates for them as well.

00:10:33.564 --> 00:10:36.147
(bright music)

